PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Most colleagues (vast majority) selected E, citing Rule 3.3-3 LSO Rules: reasonable belief of imminent harm ("tonight" with gun) permits disclosure of confidential info when necessary to prevent death/serious bodily harm. They argue the threat meets imminence, James's belief was reasonable, and the info shared (names/addresses) was tailored to police locating Eric. A/B/D rejected as inconsistent with the rule's text (not absolute bar, not mandatory on police request, threat not remote). 

Minority (colleagues 3,12) selected C: imminence prong satisfied but disclosure exceeded "necessary" limit by including children's identities + Florida vacation properties (irrelevant to locating Eric that night in Ontario; police did not request). This over-disclosure means exception not properly invoked.

Key unresolved tension: scope of "necessary" (rule text requires disclosure limited to what prevents harm). No external precedents cited; analysis rests only on rule wording + facts. My prior view aligned with E, but minority correctly flags potential overbreadth in the listed disclosures (esp. Florida properties), which may tip to C if the necessity element is strictly applied. This disagreement and exact scope issue preserved for later rounds.}

